1
00:00:01,810 --> 00:00:03,850
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:04,510
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:10,830
My name is Catherine and today we have part two of our series about small talk in English.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:11,610
That's right.

5
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:21,090
So as we mentioned, small talk are very short, quick conversations about everyday things like the weather and usually have this with a colleague very quickly or a friend maybe.

6
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:23,590
Yeah, maybe not someone you know very well too.

7
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:24,570
Exactly.

8
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:26,780
So we're going to take a look at part two.

9
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:29,210
Why don't we listen to the dialogue and see what's going on.

10
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:37,650
So Michelle, let me introduce you to Maria.

11
00:00:38,410 --> 00:00:39,370
She's my colleague from Brazil.

12
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:40,890
Maria, this is Michelle.

13
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:41,990
Hi Michelle.

14
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:44,210
So what do you do here in Shanghai?

15
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:46,390
I mean, what work do you do?

16
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:48,690
I work in advertising right now.

17
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:49,770
How about you?

18
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:51,730
I'm actually in the wine business.

19
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:53,670
Oh, that sounds really great.

20
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:55,050
I love wine myself.

21
00:00:55,910 --> 00:00:57,630
Is this your first time to Shanghai, Maria?

22
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:00,230
No, actually I often visit.

23
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:03,530
I usually come to China for business at least once a year.

24
00:01:03,890 --> 00:01:05,430
Also I love the restaurants in Shanghai.

25
00:01:06,030 --> 00:01:07,670
So that's a good reason to come.

26
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:08,650
Me too.

27
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:12,250
Actually, there's a great Brazilian restaurant I recommend.

28
00:01:12,890 --> 00:01:16,590
I mean, the food is delicious, but the service isn't so good.

29
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:20,570
I often like to get together with friends and have a great barbecue there.

30
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:22,070
We should go there together sometime.

31
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:23,370
Wonderful idea.

32
00:01:23,790 --> 00:01:24,370
I'd love that.

33
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:29,020
All right, we're back.

34
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:34,080
So now let's take a look at some of these very interesting phrases we find in this dialogue in Language Takeaway.

35
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:37,790
Language Takeaway.

36
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:43,610
So the first phrase we have today is from the first sentence of this dialogue.

37
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:48,430
James says, so Michelle, let me introduce you to Maria.

38
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:49,490
That's right.

39
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:55,260
And the second phrase, introduce someone to or introduce you to.

40
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:58,780
Okay, let me introduce you to this person.

41
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,840
So Marco, let me introduce you to my friend.

42
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:01,480
Right.

43
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:07,940
And this is something that you say when you want to tell the person, look, you're going to meet someone new.

44
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,500
Let me introduce you to my friend, Sally.

45
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:16,840
And this is very important because it's polite and it's a way for you to say, okay, you two don't know each other.

46
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:20,580
Here, this is this person's name, this is this person's name.

47
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:26,660
It's very important when you are introducing people or when you're in a situation with two people who don't know each other.

48
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:27,400
That's right.

49
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:29,180
Exactly.

50
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:30,700
So let me introduce you to someone.

51
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:42,740
Now another key question here, I think probably one of the key questions when you meet someone new, you're talking, small talk, short conversation, what do you do here or what do you do?

52
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:43,740
What do you do?

53
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:45,800
So this might sound like a very strange question.

54
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:46,820
What do you do?

55
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:47,840
Do you watch TV?

56
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:49,400
Do you like to eat food?

57
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:52,460
No, but this means what job do you do?

58
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:52,860
Right.

59
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:55,240
What type of work do you do?

60
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:55,880
Okay.

61
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:57,040
So Marco, what do you do?

62
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,060
I host an English podcast.

63
00:02:59,440 --> 00:02:59,820
All right.

64
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:01,340
Me too.

65
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:02,700
So this is the question.

66
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:07,920
Now don't get confused and remember that this is the typical way of asking someone what is their job.

67
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:14,260
If you say, what is your job or what is your occupation, what is your occupation?

68
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:16,000
It's too formal and it's kind of awkward.

69
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:18,620
I don't think anyone has ever asked me what is your occupation.

70
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:20,460
Now I rarely say that.

71
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:23,420
I almost always say, what do you do?

72
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:25,620
Or maybe what type of work do you do?

73
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:28,120
Or what business are you in?

74
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:30,460
So this is our next phrase actually.

75
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,220
Right.

76
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:39,760
If someone asks you what business are you in or what industry are you in, you would say like in this case, Maria, she is in the wine business.

77
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:40,680
Okay.

78
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,280
I'm in the wine business.

79
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:46,560
That means that my job has to do with selling or buying wine.

80
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:52,540
So if we replace the noun wine with something else, for example, clothing.

81
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:56,330
I'm in the clothing business or I'm in the cheese business.

82
00:03:56,830 --> 00:03:57,110
Exactly.

83
00:03:57,210 --> 00:03:58,630
You can basically change it with anything.

84
00:03:58,930 --> 00:03:59,630
With any product.

85
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:03,740
I guess if you sell computers, I'm in the computer business.

86
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:07,700
Or if you have anything to do with computers, you can say I'm in the computer business.

87
00:04:07,930 --> 00:04:08,250
All right.

88
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:08,890
So that's great.

89
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:10,390
I'm in the hmm business.

90
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:11,230
Exactly.

91
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:12,510
All right.

92
00:04:12,710 --> 00:04:14,110
So now we know what the person does.

93
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:16,150
We continue asking questions.

94
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:17,340
You know, we're interested.

95
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:19,790
We want to keep the conversation going a little while longer.

96
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:22,150
We say, Oh wow, that's really great.

97
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:24,020
I love wine myself.

98
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:26,140
Then we ask, is this your first time to Shanghai?

99
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:27,080
Okay.

100
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:30,900
So is this your first time to Shanghai or in Shanghai?

101
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:34,280
This means is this your first time to visit Shanghai?

102
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:35,760
That's right.

103
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:43,940
The preposition here is always confusing for an ESL learner because sometimes do you say, is this your first time in Shanghai?

104
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:45,800
Is this your first time on Shanghai?

105
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:47,600
Now we're saying to Shanghai.

106
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:51,400
So in this case or in this type of question, both are correct.

107
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:53,440
Is this your first time in Shanghai?

108
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:55,660
Is this your first time to Shanghai?

109
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:56,340
All right.

110
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:00,800
So I could also say, you know, if we're in Chicago, Hey Marco, is this your first time in Chicago?

111
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:04,880
But I have to ask if we are in that city.

112
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:09,560
I can't say, okay, I'm in Chicago today, but Marco, is this your first time in New York?

113
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:11,880
Because we're not in New York right now.

114
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:13,220
That could be confusing.

115
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:18,560
So remember when you ask this question, you're asking about where you are today, right now.

116
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:19,120
Exactly.

117
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:20,140
Very good.

118
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:22,220
So on that note, let's take a break.

119
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:25,500
Let's listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back with some more phrases.

120
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:34,040
So Michelle, let me introduce you to Maria.

121
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:35,760
She's my colleague from Brazil.

122
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:37,300
Maria, this is Michelle.

123
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:38,440
Hi, Michelle.

124
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:40,620
So what do you do here in Shanghai?

125
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:43,420
I mean, what work do you do?

126
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:45,100
I work in advertising right now.

127
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,180
How about you?

128
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:48,180
I'm actually in the wine business.

129
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:50,060
Oh, that sounds really great.

130
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:52,400
I love wine myself.

131
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,000
Is this your first time to Shanghai, Maria?

132
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:56,660
No, actually, I often visit.

133
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:59,920
I usually come to China for business at least once a year.

134
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:01,860
Also I love the restaurants in Shanghai.

135
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:04,080
So that's a good reason to come.

136
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:05,060
Me too.

137
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:08,640
Actually, there's a great Brazilian restaurant I recommend.

138
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:13,000
I mean, the food is delicious, but the service isn't so good.

139
00:06:13,470 --> 00:06:16,990
I often like to get together with friends and have a great barbecue there.

140
00:06:17,290 --> 00:06:18,470
We should go there together sometime.

141
00:06:19,010 --> 00:06:19,750
Wonderful idea.

142
00:06:20,210 --> 00:06:20,790
I'd love that.

143
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:32,560
All right, let's take a look at three more key phrases on Fluency Builder Part 2.

144
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:42,450
Fluency Builder So one phrase that we heard from this when things started to get a little bit more personal, Michelle said, I often like to get together with friends.

145
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:45,760
So this phrase here is to get together with friends.

146
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:51,450
So to get together means to be united or go with friends somewhere.

147
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:53,300
That's right.

148
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:54,830
So we could also use the phrase to hang out.

149
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:58,230
This means you're not doing anything in particular.

150
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:04,390
You're not cooking or swimming or going shopping, but it just means you're together with your friends.

151
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:05,530
You could be doing anything.

152
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:06,710
Right, exactly.

153
00:07:07,540 --> 00:07:13,530
So even if you want to ask your friend or colleague, hey, do you want to get together after work and have a couple of drinks?

154
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:17,710
All right, that means would you like to hang out or would you like to go somewhere for a drink?

155
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:20,190
It's very informal and it's not romantic.

156
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:22,410
It's just with friends.

157
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:23,030
Exactly.

158
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:27,410
So if you ask someone to get together sometime, it doesn't mean let's go out on a date.

159
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:29,420
It just means let's hang out.

160
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:30,420
Let's hang out.

161
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:31,420
Let's be friends.

162
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:32,420
Okay.

163
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:37,860
Now, so they were talking about a Brazilian restaurant and James said, hey, we should go there sometime.

164
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:42,200
Okay, we should go there together sometime is even more personal.

165
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:46,640
This is a really common phrase and one that you guys should remember as a phrase.

166
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:49,360
We should go there sometime.

167
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:53,400
That means that I would like to make plans with you to go to this restaurant.

168
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:54,460
Right, exactly.

169
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:58,140
Now, if you notice the word sometime is separated.

170
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:01,460
You have some and then apart, the other word is time.

171
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:03,400
So it's very different from sometimes.

172
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:04,820
Don't confuse it.

173
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:09,140
What we're saying here is when you have time, when I have time, we should go there.

174
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:11,760
It's almost like saying one time or in the future.

175
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:13,660
Sometimes is different.

176
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:15,360
It's like I brush my teeth sometimes.

177
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,000
That means that, not very often.

178
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:18,940
No, that's not true.

179
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:25,100
But I would like to go there sometime means, you know, it's a little bit vague.

180
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:28,100
I don't say when, but I would like to in the future.

181
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:28,700
Exactly.

182
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:30,280
And Michelle agrees.

183
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:32,700
She says, well, it's a wonderful idea.

184
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:33,640
I'd love that.

185
00:08:34,070 --> 00:08:34,330
Okay.

186
00:08:34,370 --> 00:08:35,810
This is another great phrase.

187
00:08:36,090 --> 00:08:37,370
Remember this as a phrase.

188
00:08:38,170 --> 00:08:40,090
Would you like to go see a movie?

189
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:41,450
I'd love that.

190
00:08:42,060 --> 00:08:42,190
I'd love that.

191
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:43,430
So I'd love that.

192
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:44,330
I'd love to.

193
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:46,570
These are ways of saying I like the idea.

194
00:08:47,260 --> 00:08:47,650
Let's do it.

195
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:48,430
Exactly.

196
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:53,010
So it's usually in response to an invitation or suggestion.

197
00:08:54,940 --> 00:08:55,150
Very good.

198
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:56,940
All right.

199
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:59,910
So let's take a look at our dialogue again and review everything and we'll be back in a bit.

200
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:08,230
So Michelle, let me introduce you to Maria.

201
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:09,970
She's my colleague from Brazil.

202
00:09:10,570 --> 00:09:11,490
Maria, this is Michelle.

203
00:09:12,030 --> 00:09:12,610
Hi Michelle.

204
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:14,830
So what do you do here in Shanghai?

205
00:09:15,490 --> 00:09:16,990
I mean, what work do you do?

206
00:09:17,490 --> 00:09:19,290
I work in advertising right now.

207
00:09:19,770 --> 00:09:20,390
How about you?

208
00:09:20,950 --> 00:09:22,330
I'm actually in the wine business.

209
00:09:22,970 --> 00:09:24,270
Oh, that sounds really great.

210
00:09:24,670 --> 00:09:25,630
I love wine myself.

211
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:28,230
Is this your first time to Shanghai, Maria?

212
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:30,830
No, actually, I often visit.

213
00:09:31,210 --> 00:09:34,130
I usually come to China for business at least once a year.

214
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:36,060
Also I love the restaurants in Shanghai.

215
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:38,280
So that's a good reason to come.

216
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:39,260
Me too.

217
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:42,860
Actually, there's a great Brazilian restaurant I recommend.

218
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:47,200
I mean, the food is delicious, but the service isn't so good.

219
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:51,200
I often like to get together with friends and have a great barbecue there.

220
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:52,680
We should go there together sometime.

221
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:53,960
Wonderful idea.

222
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:54,980
I'd love that.

223
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:59,100
All right.

224
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:07,160
So as we can see, it's very quick, small conversation, but very good phrases to talk to a stranger, to start up a small conversation.

225
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:17,900
I think these are great too, Marco, because if you're learning English and you want to practice your speaking skills, you're not going to have many people to talk about deep things with.

226
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:22,020
So it's great to use with strangers or with people in your office.

227
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:23,740
That's right.

228
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:25,540
And these are common, typical questions.

229
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:27,440
And they're also appropriate to ask someone.

230
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:34,000
So obviously, if you meet someone new, you're not going to ask them how much money they make because that's kind of inappropriate in Western culture.

231
00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:34,880
That's right.

232
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:38,220
So what we've taught you here are very polite, very common phrases.

233
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:41,720
We hope, hope, hope that you'll go out and practice these.

234
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:44,820
If you have questions or comments, don't hesitate to leave them for us.

235
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:46,380
Our website is EnglishPod.com.

236
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:46,900
All right.

237
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:47,540
We'll see you guys there.

238
